# cellmgr (cellctl helper)

`cellmgr` provisions and runs multiple `cellctl` cells from one host-side configuration.
It uses one shared read-only base layer (from `base.tar.xz`) and one per-cell writable data tree mounted at `/.data`.
The base layer exposes writable paths via symlinks (`/etc`, `/var`, `/tmp`, `/home`, `/root`, `/usr/pkg` -> `/.data/...`).
It can also provide optional cell-local `/dev` (tmpfs + `MAKEDEV`).

## Important command semantics

- `create <name>` creates/updates the cell config and filesystem layout.
- `create -a <name>` does the same and initializes `CELL_AUTOSTART=YES`.
- `create -x '<cmd ...>' <name>` initializes `CELL_SUPERVISE_CMD` in `cell.conf`.
- `create -l <profile>` stores the selected secmodel profile (`low`, `medium`, `high`) in `cell.conf` and forwards it to `cellctl create`.
- `create -r <port[,port...]>` reserves one or more listening ports for the cell (`cellctl create -r`).
- `create -f <facility> -o <stdout-level> -e <stderr-level> -t <tag>` initializes logging options for `cellctl supervise`.
- `start <name>` and `restart <name>` use per-cell settings from `${CELL_DATA_DIR}/<name>/cell.conf`.
- `start --all` / `stop --all` / `restart --all` work only on cells with `CELL_AUTOSTART=YES`.

## Workflow

1. Install the helper script:

   ```sh
   install -m 0755 cellmgr /usr/sbin/cellmgr
   ```

2. Create `/etc/cellmgr.conf` from the sample and customize it.

3. Bootstrap shared release content:

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr bootstrap
   ```

   During bootstrap, `cellmgr` ensures:

   - module `secmodel_cell` is loaded (`modload secmodel_cell` when needed),
   - `/etc/modules.conf` exists,
   - `/etc/modules.conf` contains a `secmodel_cell` entry for reboot persistence.
   - `/etc/rc.conf` contains `cellmgr=` (initialized to `NO` if absent).

4. Create each cell (writes `${CELL_DATA_DIR}/<name>/cell.conf`):

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr create cell1
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr create -a cell2
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr create -a -x '/bin/sleep 60' \
       -l medium -r 80,443 -f local3 -o info -e err -t cell-web cell3
   ```

   Then set at least `CELL_SUPERVISE_CMD` and `CELL_AUTOSTART` in each
   cell-local `cell.conf`.
   During creation, `cellmgr` unpacks `./etc`, `./root`, `./var`, and
   `./dev` from `etc.tar.xz` into the cell's writable `data/` tree.
   For local `/dev` setup it uses `data/dev/MAKEDEV*` from that set.


   Real-world example for cell `www`:

   ```sh
   CELL_SUPERVISE_CMD="/usr/libexec/httpd -I 8080 -X -s -f /srv/www"
   ```

5. Start cells:

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr start cell1
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr start --all
   ```

6. Restart cells:

   ```sh
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr restart cell1
   /usr/sbin/cellmgr restart --all
   ```

## Notes

- `cellmgr start` uses `cellctl create` followed by `cellctl supervise`.
- `cellctl supervise` only forwards what the supervised process writes to
  stdout/stderr. For `bozohttpd` (`/usr/libexec/httpd`), add `-s` if you want
  request/error logs to go to stderr (otherwise `bozohttpd` logs via syslog
  from inside the cell).
- By default, bootstrap derives the set URL from `RELEASE` and `MACHINE_ARCH` as:
  `https://cdn.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/NetBSD-${RELEASE}/${MACHINE_ARCH}/binary/sets`
  (override with `NETBSD_MIRROR`).
- In `/etc/cellmgr.conf`, leave `RELEASE`/`MACHINE_ARCH` empty to auto-detect
  from the running host; set them explicitly only when bootstrapping a different
  target release/arch.
- Host keys are generated by each cell's `/etc/rc` if missing.
- During `create`, host `/etc/resolv.conf` is copied to each cell's `data/etc/resolv.conf` when present.
- `/etc/rc.d/cellmgr` is installed as part of the regular base system.

- Local per-cell `/dev` setup is enabled by default (`CELL_LOCAL_DEV=YES`) and can be tuned with `CELL_DEV_SIZE` and `CELL_DEVICES` (default: `"std ptm"`).
